                                              rdfs:label "Fetal structure" .


###  https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/FMA_67498
<https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/FMA_67498> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                              rdfs:subClassOf gufo:Object ;
                                              rdfs:comment """Inherited from Foundational Model of Anatomy (FMA), has as definition \"Anatomical structure which has as its direct parts portions of two or more types of tissue or two or more types of cardinal organ part which constitute a maximally connected anatomical structure demarcated predominantly by a bona fide anatomical surface. Examples: femur, biceps, liver, heart, skin, tracheobronchial tree, ovary.\"

According to Transformation Pattern #5, it is an Object.

Transformation respected the disjointness restriction with respect to immaterial entities.""" ;
                                              rdfs:label "Organ" .


###  https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/FMA_67509
<https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/FMA_67509> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                              rdfs:subClassOf gufo:Object ;
                                              rdfs:comment """Inherited from Foundational Model of Anatomy (FMA), has as definition \"Anatomical structure, which consists of a specific set of the members of (predominantly) one organ or organ part subclass interconnected by zones of continuity or body substances; is demarcated from other subdivisions of the same organ system by one or more zones of continuity or anatomical lines; together with other subdivisions of the same organ system, it constitutes an organ system. Examples: rib cage, facial skeleton, portal system, central nervous system, upper respiratory tract, muscle group of back.\"

According to Transformation Pattern #7, it is an Object. I can be related to another gUFO:Object representing the Organ it is a component of, via a isObjectProperPartOf relation. 

It is important to recognize the possibility of Organ systems to be represented as (e.g., nervous system or urinary system) Functional Complexes - in which case the relation may be one of isComponentOf. However, this is not recommended considering the nature of Organ system subdivisions - which represent regional divisions rather than divisions made based on components.

Transformation respected the disjointness restriction with respect to immaterial entities.""" ;
                                              rdfs:label "Organ system subdivision" .


###  https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/FMA_67619
<https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/FMA_67619> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                              rdfs:subClassOf gufo:Object ;
                                              rdfs:comment """Inherited from Foundational Model of Anatomy (FMA), has as definition \"Cardinal organ part which is a fiat subdivision of an organ. Examples: lingula of left lung, tail of pancreas, artery, trochanter, nodulus of semilunar valve, right side of heart, duodenum, fundus of stomach.\"

According to Transformation Pattern #7, it is an Object. I can be related to another gUFO:Object representing the Organ it is a component of, via a isObjectProperPartOf relation.

Transformation respected the disjointness restriction with respect to immaterial entities.""" ;
                                              rdfs:label "Organ region" .


