<https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OAE_0002807> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                rdfs:subClassOf <https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OAE_0000002> ;
                                                rdfs:label "medical procedure" .


###  https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OBI_0000011
<https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OBI_0000011> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                rdfs:subClassOf <https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/BFO_0000015> ;
                                                rdfs:comment "Inherited from Ontology for Biomedical Investigations (OBI), it is defined as \"A process that realizes a plan which is the concretization of a plan specification.\"" ;
                                                rdfs:label "planned process" .


###  https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OBI_0000245
<https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OBI_0000245> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                rdfs:subClassOf gufo:FunctionalComplex ;
                                                rdfs:comment "In the original file, it is modeled as a subclass of Object Aggregate and of Material Entity. After the transformation, and because of the description of the entity as bearing members with different roles and organization rules, the resulting target class is a Functional Complex." ,
                                                             """Inherited from the Ontology for Biomedical Investigations (OBI), has as definition \"An entity that can bear roles, has members, and has a set of organization rules. Members of organizations are either organizations themselves or individual people. Members can bear specific organization member roles that are determined in the organization rules. The organization rules also determine how decisions are made on behalf of the organization by the organization members.\"

According to Transformation Pattern #5, we will consider an organization a Functional Complex that has multiple members with different roles within the organization.""" ;
                                                rdfs:label "organization" .


###  https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OBI_0001167
<https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OBI_0001167> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                rdfs:subClassOf <https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/IAO_0000416> ,
                                                                [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                  owl:onProperty <https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/IAO_0000221> ;
                                                                  owl:someValuesFrom <https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/EFO_0000246>
                                                                ] ;
                                                rdfs:label "age measurement datum" .


###  https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OBI_0001168
<https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OBI_0001168> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                rdfs:subClassOf <https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OBI_0001167> ;
                                                rdfs:label "age since fertilization measurement datum" .


###  https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OBI_0001169
<https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OBI_0001169> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                rdfs:subClassOf <https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OBI_0001167> ;
                                                rdfs:label "age since birth measurement datum" .


###  https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OBI_0100026
<https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OBI_0100026> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                rdfs:subClassOf gufo:FunctionalComplex ;
                                                rdfs:comment """Inherited from OBI, has as definition\"A material entity that is an individual living system, such as animal, plant, bacteria or virus, that is capable of replicating or reproducing, growth and maintenance in the right environment. An organism may be unicellular or made up, like humans, of many billions of cells divided into specialized tissues and organs.\"

Interpreted as a gUFO:Functional Complex, according to Transformation Pattern #5.""" ;
                                                rdfs:label "organism" .


###  https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OGMS_0000014
<https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OGMS_0000014> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                 rdfs:subClassOf <https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OGMS_0000123> ;
                                                 rdfs:label "clinical finding" .


###  https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OGMS_0000015
<https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OGMS_0000015> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                 rdfs:subClassOf <https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OGMS_0000123> ;
                                                 rdfs:label "clinical history" .


###  https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OGMS_0000020
<https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OGMS_0000020> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                 rdfs:subClassOf gufo:Quality ;
                                                 rdfs:comment """Inherited from the Ontology for General Medical Science (OGMS), it has as description \"A quality of a patient that is observed by the patient or a processual entity experienced by the patient, either of which is hypothesized by the patient to be a realization of a disease.\"

 The taxonomy of symptoms, signs in the original OntONeo is fully understood as subclasses of BFO:Quality.

Because the existing subclasses of symptom in OntONeo concern single individuals, they will be transformed, according to Transformation Pattern #9, to gUFO:Quality. However, it is understable by the definition that some subclasses would represent gUFO:Events. The existence of types in gUFO could allow for the integration of multiple symptoms of different natures to be considered as vital signs.""" ,
                                                              """The taxonomy of symptoms, inherited from the Symptom Ontology (SYMP), is fully understood in the original ontology as subclasses of BFO:Quality.

Because they concern single individuals, the symptoms in general will be transformed, according to Transformation Pattern #9, to gUFO:Quality.""" ;
                                                 rdfs:label "symptom" .


###  https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OGMS_0000024
<https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OGMS_0000024> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                 rdfs:subClassOf gufo:Quality ;
                                                 rdfs:comment """Inherited from the Ontology for General Medical Science (OGMS), it has as description \"A quality of a patient, a material entity that is part of a patient, or a processual entity that a patient participates in, any one of which is observed in a physical examination and is deemed by the clinician to be of clinical significance.\"

 The taxonomy of symptoms, signs in the original OntONeo is fully understood as subclasses of BFO:Quality.

Because the existing subclasses of sign in OntONeo concern single individuals, they will be transformed, according to Transformation Pattern #9, to gUFO:Quality. However, it is understable by the definition that some subclasses would represent gUFO:Objects or gUFO:Events. The existence of types in gUFO could allow for the integration of multiple signs of different natures to be considered as vital signs.""" ;
                                                 rdfs:label "sign" .


###  https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OGMS_0000029
<https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OGMS_0000029> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                 rdfs:subClassOf <https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OGMS_0000024> ;
                                                 rdfs:label "vital sign" .


###  https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OGMS_0000031
<https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OGMS_0000031> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                 rdfs:subClassOf gufo:Aspect ;
                                                 rdfs:comment """Inherited from the Ontology for General Medical Science (OGMS), it has as definition \"A disposition (i) to undergo pathological processes that (ii) exists in an organism because of one or more disorders in that organism.\"

In the orginal OntONeo, the disease taxonomy is imported as subclasses of a Disposition. However, one has to assess case by case whether the disease depends on the single individual in which it inheres (e.g., a genetic malformation) or if it depends on other individuals (e.g. a viral infection). For this reason, and according to Transformation Pattern #11, disease has gUFO:Aspect as target hierarchy.""" ;
                                                 rdfs:label "disease" .


###  https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OGMS_0000045
<https://www.example.org/ontoneo-u/OGMS_0000045> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                 rdfs:subClassOf gufo:Object ;
                                                 rdfs:comment """This concept is inherited from Ontology for General Medical Science (OGMS), and has as definition \"A material entity which is clinically abnormal and part of an extended organism. Disorders are the physical basis of disease.\"

According to Transformation Pattern #5, it is an Object. I can be related to a Functional Complex representing the organism that it is a component of.

Transformation respected the disjointness restriction with respect to immaterial entities.""" ;
                                                 rdfs:label "disorder" .


